----------------------------------------
- User Defined Classes (Introduction) -
----------------------------------------

In an object oriented programming language like Java, we can also create our own classes. We do this in Java by writing 
a class definition. Here is a simple definition for a class named Bunny.

		class Bunny {
		}

Note that a class defines a type of object, and objects are things; therefore our class names should generally be nouns 
or noun-like.

A basic class definition looks like this in Java:

		class <ClassName> {
   		    <classBody>
		}

There are three parts to a basic class definition:

   1.  class the keyword class
   2.  <ClassName> the name of the class being defined
   3.  <classBody> the class body, surrounded by curly braces { }

A class definition creates a new data type, so by defining a class named Bunny we have created a Bunny data type. 
This means we can now declare variables of type Bunny.

		Bunny myBunny;
		Bunny yourBunny;

*** A class definition creates a new data type. ***

We can even instantiate some Bunny objects and assign them to our Bunny variables.

		myBunny = new Bunny();
		yourBunny = new Bunny();

Unfortunately, we cannot really do anything else with our Bunny objects right now, because they have no attributes and 
no operations - they know nothing and can do nothing.
